  "step_1": "The decision reason considers whether each lottery contains a sure outcome (i.e., an outcome that occurs with 100% probability). In Lottery A, the outcomes are 3000 Euros with 0.2% probability and 0 Euros with 99.8% probability. In Lottery B, the outcomes are 6000 Euros with 0.1% probability and 0 Euros with 99.9% probability. Neither lottery has a sure outcome, as both involve only probabilistic outcomes (no outcome occurs with 100% probability). Therefore, the condition of the decision reason — the presence of a sure outcome in each lottery — does not apply, because neither lottery contains a sure outcome. The reason would be indifferent if it only compares presence of sure outcomes, but its preference rule (favoring the lottery without a sure outcome) cannot differentiate between A and B here, since both lack a sure outcome.",
